Loading performance gets all the attention; **runtime** performance is what people actually feel after the page has loaded. And it all comes back to one fact: there is one main thread, and it renders, runs your JavaScript, and handles input.

## The budget

At 60 fps you have **16.7 ms per frame**, of which the browser needs several for style, layout, paint and composite. You get roughly **10 ms of JavaScript per frame.**

A task longer than 50 ms is a **long task**: input during it is queued, not handled. A 300 ms task is a 300 ms freeze — the page does not respond to a tap, and the user taps again.

## INP — the metric that measures this

**Interaction to Next Paint** measures the full latency from an interaction to the next frame that reflects it, across the whole visit. Good is under 200 ms at p75.

It has three parts, and knowing which one is yours is the whole debugging strategy:

```
[ input delay ] [ processing ] [ presentation delay ]
       ▲               ▲                 ▲
  the main thread   your handler    style, layout,
  was already       ran long        paint were expensive
  busy
```

| Part | Cause | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Input delay** | A long task was already running | Break up the work — this is the most common cause |
| **Processing** | Your handler does too much | Do the minimum synchronously; defer the rest |
| **Presentation** | Huge DOM, expensive layout | Reduce nodes, avoid layout thrash, use `content-visibility` |

## Breaking up long tasks

```javascript
// One 800 ms task: the page is frozen for 800 ms.
for (const item of tenThousandItems) process(item);

// Yield between chunks so input can be handled in the gaps.
async function processAll(items) {
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i += 50) {
    items.slice(i, i + 50).forEach(process);
    // scheduler.yield() where available — it resumes at the front of
    // the queue rather than the back, so the work does not starve.
    await (scheduler.yield?.() ?? new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0)));
  }
}
```

**`scheduler.postTask`** lets you say how important the work is:

```javascript
scheduler.postTask(renderResults, { priority: 'user-blocking' });
scheduler.postTask(prefetchNext, { priority: 'background' });
```

## Respond first, work second

The single highest-leverage pattern for INP:

```javascript
button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  // 1 · Paint the acknowledgement immediately. This is what INP measures.
  setPending(true);
  // 2 · Let the browser paint before doing the expensive thing.
  await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(() => setTimeout(r, 0)));
  // 3 · Now do the work.
  const result = await expensiveThing();
  setResult(result);
});
```

The user sees a response in one frame. The work still takes as long, but the interaction is no longer what is slow.

## Layout thrash — the classic bug

```javascript
// Read, write, read, write… each read forces a synchronous layout
// because the previous write invalidated it. O(n) layouts.
for (const el of elements) {
  el.style.height = `${el.offsetHeight * 2}px`;
}

// Batch: all reads, then all writes. One layout.
const heights = elements.map((el) => el.offsetHeight);
elements.forEach((el, i) => { el.style.height = `${heights[i] * 2}px`; });
```

The properties that force layout when read — `offsetTop`, `offsetHeight`, `scrollTop`, `getBoundingClientRect`, `getComputedStyle` — are worth knowing by name, because one of them inside a loop is the most common runtime performance bug there is.

## Animate on the compositor

```css
/* Composited: no layout, no paint, runs off the main thread. */
transform: translateX(100px);
opacity: 0.5;

/* Triggers layout on every frame. Never animate these. */
left: 100px;
width: 200px;
```

Only `transform` and `opacity` (and `filter`) can be handled by the compositor. `will-change: transform` promotes an element to its own layer — but **each layer costs memory**, so applying it broadly makes things worse, not better.

## React-specific costs

```javascript
// Every render creates a new object → every memoised child re-renders.
<Child style={{ margin: 8 }} onSelect={(id) => pick(id)} />

// Stable references let memoisation actually work.
const style = useMemo(() => ({ margin: 8 }), []);
const onSelect = useCallback((id) => pick(id), [pick]);
```

And the bigger lever: **move state down** rather than memoising around it. A component that owns state only its subtree needs does not re-render its siblings, and needs no memo at all. Composition beats memoisation.

`useTransition` marks an update as interruptible, so typing stays responsive while an expensive list re-renders:

```javascript
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
setQuery(value);                                  // urgent — the input
startTransition(() => setResults(filter(value))); // interruptible
```

## Web Workers

Anything genuinely CPU-bound — parsing large JSON, image processing, search indexing, diffing — belongs off the main thread. The cost is the `postMessage` boundary: data is structured-cloned, which for a large object is itself expensive. Use `Transferable` objects or `SharedArrayBuffer` when the payload is large.

## Measuring

```javascript
// Long tasks, as they happen.
new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
  for (const entry of list.getEntries()) {
    if (entry.duration > 50) report('long-task', entry.duration, entry.attribution);
  }
}).observe({ type: 'longtask', buffered: true });
```

In DevTools, the **Performance panel** is the tool: look for the red-cornered long tasks, then expand to see whether the time is scripting, style, layout or paint. Each points at a different fix.

## Common mistakes

- **Optimising load and ignoring runtime.** A fast-loading page that freezes on every click.
- **A layout read inside a write loop.**
- **Animating `left`/`top`/`width`.**
- **`will-change` everywhere.** Memory pressure and worse performance.
- **Memoising instead of moving state down.**
- **Doing the work before painting the acknowledgement.**
- **Blocking the main thread with `JSON.parse` of a large payload.**

## Key takeaways

- One main thread, ~10 ms of JS per frame; a task over 50 ms is felt.
- INP splits into input delay, processing and presentation — diagnose which before fixing.
- Respond in one frame, then do the work.
- Batch reads before writes; animate only `transform` and `opacity`.
- Move state down before reaching for memo; move CPU work to a worker.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"INP is 600 ms. Where do you look?"** — The breakdown. Mostly input delay means a long task elsewhere; mostly processing means the handler; mostly presentation means the DOM or layout.
- **"A list of 10,000 items is slow to filter."** — Filter in a worker or with a transition, virtualize the render, and debounce the input.
- **"Does `React.memo` fix this?"** — Sometimes, and it is rarely the first move. Stable props and moving state down are cheaper and do not need maintenance.

Every layer is easy to write to and hard to correct. The part interviewers probe is never "would you cache this" — it is "how do you know when it is wrong, and how do you fix it once it is on someone's device."

