    {
      "note": "Finally, invalidate so the next read comes from the server. Rollback restores what you believed; only a refetch restores what is true.",
      "cells": { "ui": ["12", "unliked", "—"], "log": ["rolled back", "invalidated — refetch on next read"] },
      "marks": { "ui": { "likeCount": "done" }, "net": { "reconciled": "done" } },
      "vars": { "server truth": "12", "user sees": "12" }
    }
  ],
  "result": "Snapshot, cancel, apply, roll back, tell the user, invalidate. Skipping any one of them leaves the UI lying."
}
```

## The implementation

```javascript
const like = useMutation({
  mutationFn: (id) => api.like(id, { idempotencyKey: keyFor(id) }),

  onMutate: async (id) => {
    // 1 · Cancel in-flight refetches, or one lands after your optimistic
    //     write and silently overwrites it.
    await qc.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['post', id] });
    // 2 · Snapshot. This is the only way back.
    const prev = qc.getQueryData(['post', id]);
    // 3 · Apply.
    qc.setQueryData(['post', id], (p) => ({ ...p, likeCount: p.likeCount + 1, liked: true }));
    return { prev };
  },

  // 4 · Roll back, and say something.
  onError: (_err, id, ctx) => {
    qc.setQueryData(['post', id], ctx.prev);
    toast('Could not like that post');
  },

  // 5 · Reconcile with the server either way.
  onSettled: (_d, _e, id) => qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['post', id] }),
});
```

Step 1 is the one people omit and the one that produces the worst bug: a refetch that was already in flight resolves with the old value *after* your optimistic write, and the UI silently reverts with no error anywhere.

## When to be optimistic, and when not

| Optimistic | Not optimistic |
| --- | --- |
| Likes, reactions, stars | Payments |
| Marking read, archiving | Anything with legal or financial weight |
| Reordering, renaming | Anything that cannot be undone |
| Adding a comment | Anything whose failure is expensive to explain |
| Toggling a setting | Anything needing server-computed output |

The rule: **be optimistic when failure is rare and the rollback is cheap to explain.** "Your like didn't save" is fine. "Your payment didn't go through, and also we already showed you a receipt" is not.

## A normalised cache makes this work everywhere

Because the cache is keyed by entity, one optimistic update corrects every list that shows that post — the feed, search results, the detail page. With duplicated nested copies you would have to find and update all three, and you will miss one.

## Conflict resolution — the follow-up

The server has moved on since you acted. Four strategies, and the right answer is **per field, not per record**:

| Strategy | Behaviour | Right for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Last write wins** | Newest timestamp overwrites | Low-stakes single-owner fields — a title, a setting |
| **Server wins** | Discard the local change | Data the client should not own — price, permissions |
| **Merge by operation** | Replay the *intent* onto the new state | Appends and counters — comments, likes |
| **CRDT** | Converges by construction | Genuine concurrent editing |
| **Ask** | Show both and let the user choose | When a merge would destroy work |

> "Last-write-wins on a document title is fine. On the body of a shared document it silently destroys someone's work. I'd choose per field rather than per record, and where a merge would lose work, I'd show the conflict rather than pick a winner."

### Optimistic concurrency control

```javascript
// Send the version you believed you were editing. The server rejects
// the write if someone else got there first — no silent overwrite.
PATCH /documents/42
If-Match: "v12"
→ 412 Precondition Failed
```

This is how you get "someone else edited this" instead of "your colleague's work vanished".

## Rendering pending state honestly

```javascript
// The user must be able to tell what has landed and what has not.
<Comment className={comment.pending ? 'is-pending' : ''} />
```

Subdued styling, a small spinner, or "Sending…" — and a clear failed state with a retry. Hiding the distinction is what makes users stop trusting an app after one bad sync.

## Common mistakes

- **No `cancelQueries`.** A stale refetch overwrites the optimistic write.
- **No snapshot.** Nothing to roll back to.
- **Silent rollback.** The user thinks they mis-clicked.
- **Optimistic payments.**
- **No `invalidateQueries` afterwards.** The rollback restores what you believed, not what is true.
- **Last-write-wins on collaborative text.**
- **Pending items that look identical to committed ones.**

## Key takeaways

- Cancel, snapshot, apply, roll back with a message, then invalidate — all five.
- A normalised cache means one update corrects every view of that entity.
- Be optimistic where failure is rare and cheap to explain; never for money.
- Resolve conflicts per field, and show a conflict UI when merging would lose work.
- `If-Match` version tokens turn a silent overwrite into a visible conflict.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"Two optimistic updates to the same entity, back to back?"** — Each snapshots the state it saw, so rollbacks must unwind in order. Simpler and safer: serialise mutations per entity key.
- **"The user goes offline mid-mutation?"** — The mutation goes to a durable outbox and replays on reconnect with its idempotency key. See the offline entry.
- **"How do you test rollback?"** — Force the mutation to reject and assert both the restored value and that the user was told. The second assertion is the one that usually is not written.
